Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:23:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] drm/asahi: g15: renumber the compute stamp before it
 wraps
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The compute stamp of ordinal n is stamp_seed(COMPUTE_SEED, n): it steps
by 0x100 from 0xc1000000 and, after 0x3f0000 computes (4128768: about
390 FLUX.3 1024x1024 images at one node per submission), goes from
0xffffff00 back to 0. Whether the firmware takes that wrap has not been
tried either; the TA/FS renumbering of the previous commits does not
cover it.

Renumber the compute stamp the same way. Add an offset to the compute
ordinal used for the stamp and, when g15_compute_reseed_margin is set,
at the first publication with nothing in flight inside that margin
write the compute stamps back as ComputeGraph::init() leaves them with
no stamp base (user COMPUTE_SEED, firmware 0), reset the compute target
and move the offset so the next compute counts as ordinal 1. Inside the
margin nothing is published behind a flight, as for TA/FS, so the
flights drain; the margin is at least one prefix of computes
(COMPUTE_SLOTS) and at most half the stamp range. The ordinal itself,
which the compute image also carries, keeps counting as the render draw
does.

g15_compute_stamp_base starts the count near the wrap, to test this in
seconds instead of hours. Both parameters default to 0, which keeps the
stamp exactly as before.

On a J516C, with g15_compute_stamp_base=4023768 (105000 computes before
the wrap) and g15_compute_reseed_margin=100000, the stamp was renumbered
at compute 5000, the GPU kept running computes up to 159899, past the
point where the stamp would have wrapped, and a FLUX.3 1024x1024 image
generated fine afterwards.

